          <figcaption>Leong et al., <em>The Lancet</em> 2015: 139,691 adults aged 35 to 70, 17 countries, median follow-up 4.0 years. Associations from observational data. Bars scaled to the printed percentages.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <p>The authors say further research is needed to test whether improving strength reduces mortality (Leong et al., <em>The Lancet</em> 2015;386:266-273, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/S0140-6736(14)62000-6" rel="nofollow noopener">doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(14)62000-6</a>).</p>

        <h2>A Second, Larger Cohort: UK Biobank</h2>
        <p>A 2018 BMJ analysis of 502,293 UK Biobank participants aged 40 to 69 (54% women) recorded 13,322 deaths over a mean of 7.1 years. Per 5 kg lower grip, all-cause mortality hazard ratios were 1.20 in women and 1.16 in men, and cardiovascular hazard ratios were 1.19 in women and 1.22 in men (Celis-Morales et al., <em>BMJ</em> 2018;361:k1651, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.k1651" rel="nofollow noopener">doi:10.1136/bmj.k1651</a>).</p>

        <h2>Grip, Brain and Aging Markers</h2>
        <p>Grip has also been linked with brain and cellular aging measures. The table gives each finding with its design, so the strength of each claim is visible.</p>

        <p class="table-cue">Scroll the table horizontally on smaller screens.</p><div class="table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Grip strength research findings" tabindex="0"><table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Study</th>
              <th scope="col">Design and sample</th>
              <th scope="col">Finding</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="https://doi.org/10.3389/fnagi.2021.625551" rel="nofollow noopener">Cui et al. 2021</a></td>
              <td>15 longitudinal cohort studies</td>
              <td>Poorer grip was associated with cognitive decline (HR 1.99, 1.71 to 2.32) and dementia (HR 1.54, 1.32 to 1.79).</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="https://doi.org/10.1093/schbul/sby034" rel="nofollow noopener">Firth et al. 2018</a></td>
              <td>Cross-sectional, 476,559 UK Biobank participants</td>
              <td>Grip was significantly associated with cognitive functioning, particularly working memory and processing speed.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><a href="https://doi.org/10.1002/jcsm.13110" rel="nofollow noopener">Peterson et al. 2023</a></td>
              <td>US Health and Retirement Study, 2006 to 2008 waves</td>
              <td>In men, lower normalized grip was associated with faster DNA-methylation age acceleration in a cross-sectional analysis (DunedinPoAm beta -0.36, P &lt; 0.001); women showed a similar association. The authors call this "some initial evidence".</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>

        <p>The Firth study is cross-sectional, so it cannot say which came first. A 2019 narrative review on grip strength as a biomarker in older adults (Bohannon, <em>Clinical Interventions in Aging</em>) describes grip strength as "largely consistent as an explanator of concurrent overall strength, upper limb function, bone mineral density, fractures, falls, malnutrition, cognitive impairment, depression, sleep problems, diabetes, multimorbidity, and quality of life" and says routine use "can be recommended as a stand-alone measurement".</p>

        <h2>Peter Attia's Dead Hang Targets</h2>
        <p>On his podcast (The Drive, AMA #329, December 2024) Peter Attia said a 'very strong' 40-year-old man should be able to dead hang for at least two minutes, and a 'very strong' 40-year-old woman for at least 90 seconds. Attia said to subtract about 10 seconds per decade.</p>
        <p>Attia's targets are an aspiration for very strong people, not an average and not a rule for all men in their 40s. It is a podcast statement, not a research result, and no study cited here tests it. Compare your own time with measured samples on the <a href="/dead-hang-time-by-age/">dead hang time by age</a> page.</p>

        <h2>What the Research Does Not Show</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>No trial on this page tests whether hanging, or any grip training, lengthens life.</li>
          <li>The mortality studies measured grip strength, not hang time, so they give no hang time that predicts lifespan.</li>
          <li>Observational data cannot rule out other explanations, such as overall health or activity level.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A reasonable reading: grip is a cheap marker of general strength and health, and a weak grip is worth noticing.</p>

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>How does grip strength predict longevity?</h3>
          <p>A 2015 Lancet study of 139,691 adults in 17 countries found that each 5 kg lower grip strength was associated with a 16% higher risk of death from any cause. A 2018 BMJ study of 502,293 UK Biobank participants reported the same direction of association. These are observational studies, so they show a link, not that improving grip lengthens life.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>What is a good dead hang time for longevity?</h3>
          <p>There is no trial-tested longevity target. On his podcast (The Drive, AMA #329, December 2024) Peter Attia said a 'very strong' 40-year-old man should manage at least two minutes and a 'very strong' 40-year-old woman at least 90 seconds, subtracting about 10 seconds per decade. This is his target for very strong people, not an average.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq">
          <h3>Does dead hanging make you live longer?</h3>
          <p>No study has shown that dead hanging lengthens life. The mortality studies on this page measured grip strength, not hang time, and the authors of the Lancet study say further research is needed to test whether improving strength reduces mortality.</p>
        </div>

